(6758524, 1011110, 67258, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club. <br/> <br/>This is a strong photo that has immediate impact on the viewer and it meets the challenge entirely and unambiguously.<br/> <br/>On a technical level it is very well done, with a depth of field that suits the composition, and with good lighting.  I am glad you included your setup notes - your work has paid off.  The vignette adds hugely to the overall ''feel'' of the image, lending it a dark and brooding atmosphere entirely in keeping with your subject.  There is nothing that I would change in this photo - every element contributes to the whole.<br/> <br/>Little more that I can say, other than ''Good Work''!</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club.  This is a strong photo that has immediate impact on the viewer and it meets the challenge entirely and unambiguously. On a technical level it is very well done, with a depth of field that suits the composition, and with good lighting.  I am glad you included your setup notes - your work has paid off.  The vignette adds hugely to the overall ''feel'' of the image, lending it a dark and brooding atmosphere entirely in keeping with your subject.  There is nothing that I would change in this photo - every element contributes to the whole. Little more that I can say, other than ''Good Work''!', '2012-05-03 09:28:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(6758577, 1011370, 34192, '<td valign="top">very excellent!</td>', 'very excellent!', '2012-05-03 10:53:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(6759294, 1011055, 60862, '<td valign="top">Well, that''s what ya get for being creative! :)</td>', 'Well, that''s what ya get for being creative! :)', '2012-05-04 01:19:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(6759718, 1010942, 37719, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!  I have studied your image and have the following to offer:<br/> <br/>Composition/perspective: The subject for the challenge leaves few available options for a perspective so the compositional elements should be stronger in comparison to off-set that limitation.  Here, the focus of the photograph has to be the drops where your effect takes place otherwise it is just an out of focus picture of a face card.  Here is a weak point in your composition I feel.  The area of effect is so small in relation to the image size it lessens its impact.<br/> <br/>Color: the colors outside of white appear ok.  But just ok.  Seems a boost in saturation and a correction to the white point to bring out the whites would have helped in this area.  It appears to look like it is ''dingy'' - like a white tee shirt washed with the dark clothes does. (Hope that makes sense)<br/> <br/>Light:  there is no notes on your image so I have no idea how you lit the shot.  However, it does appear the light just is not bright enough.  Brighter would have helped bring out the whites and allowed a faster shutter speed to help with focus and clarity in the drops.<br/> <br/>Challenge:  As the challenge description went this certainly meets that requirement.  However, in a challenge such as this creativity matched with near perfect execution is necessary to place well.  The out of focus drops - or more correctly the image reflected in the droplets - and the less than adequate lighting takes away from the execution and the composition adds to that loss.<br/> <br/>Overall/my opinion:  I feel that a tighter crop around the drops would have been much better.  The background would still clearly be a playing card and the reflection would support that.  I think the reversed reflection is where the strength of this shot lies, better focus and perhaps closer camera placement would have helped to emphasize these small areas.  Even larger water drops would have helped in this regard.  I do applaud your attempt at this shot in the kitchen sink with the faucet dripping though.<br/> <br/>I hope this helps.  If you have any questions feel free to contact me for answers.</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club!  I have studied your image and have the following to offer: Composition/perspective: The subject for the challenge leaves few available options for a perspective so the compositional elements should be stronger in comparison to off-set that limitation.  Here, the focus of the photograph has to be the drops where your effect takes place otherwise it is just an out of focus picture of a face card.  Here is a weak point in your composition I feel.  The area of effect is so small in relation to the image size it lessens its impact. Color: the colors outside of white appear ok.  But just ok.  Seems a boost in saturation and a correction to the white point to bring out the whites would have helped in this area.  It appears to look like it is ''dingy'' - like a white tee shirt washed with the dark clothes does. (Hope that makes sense) Light:  there is no notes on your image so I have no idea how you lit the shot.  However, it does appear the light just is not bright enough.  Brighter would have helped bring out the whites and allowed a faster shutter speed to help with focus and clarity in the drops. Challenge:  As the challenge description went this certainly meets that requirement.  However, in a challenge such as this creativity matched with near perfect execution is necessary to place well.  The out of focus drops - or more correctly the image reflected in the droplets - and the less than adequate lighting takes away from the execution and the composition adds to that loss. Overall/my opinion:  I feel that a tighter crop around the drops would have been much better.  The background would still clearly be a playing card and the reflection would support that.  I think the reversed reflection is where the strength of this shot lies, better focus and perhaps closer camera placement would have helped to emphasize these small areas.  Even larger water drops would have helped in this regard.  I do applaud your attempt at this shot in the kitchen sink with the faucet dripping though. I hope this helps.  If you have any questions feel free to contact me for answers.', '2012-05-04 13:57:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(6759722, 1011403, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Hmmm...the main problem for me here is less the concept and more the execution.  The high ISO is super-visible and where the mirror ball catches the light directly, it lools like a blank card.  The drawings on the cards are a good idea though I honestly don''t know where the hanged figure fits in...the joker, maybe? :-)<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Hmmm...the main problem for me here is less the concept and more the execution.  The high ISO is super-visible and where the mirror ball catches the light directly, it lools like a blank card.  The drawings on the cards are a good idea though I honestly don''t know where the hanged figure fits in...the joker, maybe? :-) Feel free to PM me Susan', '2012-05-04 14:02:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(6759729, 1011284, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>This image should have placed a lot higher, imho, just on the basis of technical difficulty alone.  Very good shot, like the use of overlays.  Love the sharp focus on the hands and the blur of the cards.  Wish I could do that, I just make a mess :-)<br/> <br/>The only thing I''m not so crazy about is the strong saturation - to my eye it''s bordering on cartoony.  But other than that a very good shot, and the setup you used sounds so much like many of mine I can pretty much see it.  Keep up the good work!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! This image should have placed a lot higher, imho, just on the basis of technical difficulty alone.  Very good shot, like the use of overlays.  Love the sharp focus on the hands and the blur of the cards.  Wish I could do that, I just make a mess :-) The only thing I''m not so crazy about is the strong saturation - to my eye it''s bordering on cartoony.  But other than that a very good shot, and the setup you used sounds so much like many of mine I can pretty much see it.  Keep up the good work! Feel free to PM me Susan', '2012-05-04 14:06:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(6759740, 1011308, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Interesting array of comments here and I certainly agree with many of them - the surreal, eerie quality you''ve captured is wonderful.  At first I didn''t *get* the clock, I thought it was a ball until I read your comments.  Very good comp, the off-centred subject adds to the unsettling feeling here.  I do think that the Queen should be spot-coloured red just to drive home that it''s the Queen of Hearts.  I think that with just more of a closed-down shutter (and tripod) to help increase the dof and more importantly, kill the flat white on the Rabbit''s head and clock, you would have finished in the ribbons.  I second pretty much everything <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=11854" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Kelli</a> suggests.<br/> <br/>Very good work, congrats on the score and placing. Keep it up and I look forward to seeing more entries from you!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Interesting array of comments here and I certainly agree with many of them - the surreal, eerie quality you''ve captured is wonderful.  At first I didn''t *get* the clock, I thought it was a ball until I read your comments.  Very good comp, the off-centred subject adds to the unsettling feeling here.  I do think that the Queen should be spot-coloured red just to drive home that it''s the Queen of Hearts.  I think that with just more of a closed-down shutter (and tripod) to help increase the dof and more importantly, kill the flat white on the Rabbit''s head and clock, you would have finished in the ribbons.  I second pretty much everything  Kelli suggests. Very good work, congrats on the score and placing. Keep it up and I look forward to seeing more entries from you! Feel free to PM me Susan', '2012-05-04 14:13:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(6759899, 1011403, 50695, '<td valign="top">The hanged man is from Tarot. So is the Cup.</td>', 'The hanged man is from Tarot. So is the Cup.', '2012-05-04 18:09:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(6759989, 1011411, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>And welcome back from a long absence, too...five years without DPC?  How on earth did you survive, inquiring minds want to know...;-)<br/> <br/>Ok enough silliness.  I really like this shot, especially with the jokers in the front row.  No wonder the poor queen looks trepidatious about going out on the stage. The towel as stage curtain and checkerboard shows resourcefulness and creativity and the checkerboard also evokes Alice in Wonderland.<br/> <br/>Technically the comp is fine, good use of a tripod, long exposure, candle and a flashlight.  Done that more than once and still do!  I do feel the queen is oversharpened and a bit too bleedy to the lower right, I''m guessing that''s where the candle was located.  Very good creation of an audience in ramped seats staggered as they are in an actual theatre.<br/> <br/>Otherwise I really can''t poke any holes in this image.  I love homemade setups like this, and am glad to see you finished quite well.  So welcome back and hope to see more entries from you soon!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! And welcome back from a long absence, too...five years without DPC?  How on earth did you survive, inquiring minds want to know...;-) Ok enough silliness.  I really like this shot, especially with the jokers in the front row.  No wonder the poor queen looks trepidatious about going out on the stage. The towel as stage curtain and checkerboard shows resourcefulness and creativity and the checkerboard also evokes Alice in Wonderland. Technically the comp is fine, good use of a tripod, long exposure, candle and a flashlight.  Done that more than once and still do!  I do feel the queen is oversharpened and a bit too bleedy to the lower right, I''m guessing that''s where the candle was located.  Very good creation of an audience in ramped seats staggered as they are in an actual theatre. Otherwise I really can''t poke any holes in this image.  I love homemade setups like this, and am glad to see you finished quite well.  So welcome back and hope to see more entries from you soon! Feel free to PM me Susan', '2012-05-04 21:17:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(6759993, 1011308, 25166, '<td valign="top"><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1013779" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/25000-29999/25166/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1013779.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/25000-29999/25166/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1013779.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/25000-29999/25166/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1013779.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>- this was my first edit! I do tend to post what I want to see rather than consider the viewer. I worried that the ''colourful''card would detract from the Silver Halide vibe I so love. The original image was reasonably sharp given I shot at f/1.2, or at least the rabbit was, but I suddenly decided I liked the really blurry Len Baby shot I took during the same shoot - it had a very lofi feel to it, but would have been at  risk of a brown ribbon.....<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1011929" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/25000-29999/25166/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1011929.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/25000-29999/25166/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1011929.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/25000-29999/25166/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1011929.jpg"/></a></td>', '  - this was my first edit! I do tend to post what I want to see rather than consider the viewer. I worried that the ''colourful''card would detract from the Silver Halide vibe I so love. The original image was reasonably sharp given I shot at f/1.2, or at least the rabbit was, but I suddenly decided I liked the really blurry Len Baby shot I took during the same shoot - it had a very lofi feel to it, but would have been at  risk of a brown ribbon..... ', '2012-05-04 21:20:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(6759998, 1011394, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Made me smile, an easy 7 in voting.  The shredded cards on a cutting board are a great idea and the knife, although sheathed, adds some menace to the scene. I might have tried to jumble the cards a little more, like mixed a few of the faces together, especially as they''re all of the same suit, just to add to the chaos here.  Throw off the viewer a little and make them go ''Waitaminute...'' and come back to look at it some more.<br/> <br/>But the knife being at the top also makes it a bit top-heavy, I think if it had been at the bottom you could have pulled a lot more off. Your light source is quite strong and fairly low, I''m guessing at an overhead flood, and that gave you those grievously strong shadows under the knife and to some extent, under the cards too.  Remember that it''s an Advanced challenge and never be afraid to dodge out shadows, as I don''t think a reflector or diffuser would have helped much here.<br/> <br/>But still, I love the idea here and use of handy-dandy tools around the house!  And never fear, there is nothing wrong with entering a shot you like.  Look at DPC as a gym, where people come to work out, and focus on developing muscles according to their own goals as opposed to the artificial ideals imposed by others.  You''re doing fine, just keep at it.<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Made me smile, an easy 7 in voting.  The shredded cards on a cutting board are a great idea and the knife, although sheathed, adds some menace to the scene. I might have tried to jumble the cards a little more, like mixed a few of the faces together, especially as they''re all of the same suit, just to add to the chaos here.  Throw off the viewer a little and make them go ''Waitaminute...'' and come back to look at it some more. But the knife being at the top also makes it a bit top-heavy, I think if it had been at the bottom you could have pulled a lot more off. Your light source is quite strong and fairly low, I''m guessing at an overhead flood, and that gave you those grievously strong shadows under the knife and to some extent, under the cards too.  Remember that it''s an Advanced challenge and never be afraid to dodge out shadows, as I don''t think a reflector or diffuser would have helped much here. But still, I love the idea here and use of handy-dandy tools around the house!  And never fear, there is nothing wrong with entering a shot you like.  Look at DPC as a gym, where people come to work out, and focus on developing muscles according to their own goals as opposed to the artificial ideals imposed by others.  You''re doing fine, just keep at it. Feel free to PM me, Susan', '2012-05-04 21:29:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(6796066, 1011069, 28710, '<td valign="top">Great idea and nicely executed. I think what really makes this work is the balance of light between the cards in the foreground and the cards in the background. It helps the front cards really stand out.<br/> <br/>Excellent.</td>', 'Great idea and nicely executed. I think what really makes this work is the balance of light between the cards in the foreground and the cards in the background. It helps the front cards really stand out. Excellent.', '2012-06-15 12:06:19.000000', NULL, 0),
